<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodosius II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">401–450</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodosius II was emperor of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> from 408 to 450, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadius">Arcadius</a> as a child. His reign saw the completion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walls-of-constantinople">Theodosian Walls</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the founding of a school of higher learning in the capital and the Theodosian Code of 438, the first official collection of imperial laws. He paid large tributes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> and died after a riding accident.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii.jpg" alt="Theodosius II" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodosius_II_Louvre_Ma1036.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Theodosius II was born on 10 April 401, the only son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadius">Arcadius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelia-eudoxia">Aelia Eudoxia</a>. His father proclaimed him co-augustus on 10 January 402, at nine months, the youngest to bear the imperial title up to then. When Arcadius died on 1 May 408, the seven-year-old boy became sole emperor of the Eastern half of the empire. The praetorian prefect Anthemius governed at first, and the Theodosian walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> were built under his supervision. In 414 his older sister Pulcheria, who had vowed perpetual virginity, was proclaimed augusta and acted as his guardian. In June 421 he married Aelia Eudocia, a woman of Athenian origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the Western emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> died in 423 and Joannes was proclaimed in his place, Theodosius made war on him in 424 and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii">Valentinian III</a> in the West on 23 October 425. In 425 he founded the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, with 31 chairs, 15 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and 16 in Greek. In 429 he appointed a commission to collect the laws since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a>; a second commission completed the Codex Theodosianus, published in 438 and later a basis for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>'s Corpus Juris Civilis.</p><p>Against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> he paid 350 pounds of gold a year from 424, doubled to 700 pounds in 439 with the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bleda">Bleda</a>. After the Huns sacked the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> cities in 441 and defeated Roman armies in 447, a peace brought an annual tribute of 2,100 pounds of gold. Theodosius also made war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> in 421 to 422, which ended in stalemate.</p><p>He frequently tried to settle disputes about the nature of Christ. He appointed Nestorius archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 428 and called the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in 431, which condemned Nestorius. The Second Council of Ephesus in 449 restored Eutyches and deposed Flavian, and Pope Leo I called it the robber synod; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> reversed it after the emperor's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Theodosius died on 28 July 450 after falling from his horse, and was buried in the Church of the Holy Apostles. Pulcheria married Marcian, who became emperor. Ancient and modern historians often see Theodosius as pushed around by his sister, his wife and the eunuch Chrysaphius, whose influence on foreign policy towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> and on the pro-Monophysite line was resented by Pulcheria.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theodosius II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Theodosian Walls, whose main line was completed in 413 CE under <b>Theodosius II</b> and which were strengthened after an earthquake in 447 with an outer wall and moat, made the city effectively impregnable to any army that lacked both command of the surrounding waters and the means…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Romans breached the Treaty of Margus in 440, Attila and Bleda razed Margus, Singidunum and Viminacium; in 443, after defeating the Romans at the Battle of Chersonesus, they forced <b>Theodosius II</b> to the Peace of Anatolius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When he raided Sicily in 440 the Western Empire was too occupied with war in Gaul to react, and an expedition sent by <b>Theodosius II</b> in 441 got no further than Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A second church, built by the architect Rufinus, was inaugurated by <b>Theodosius II</b> in 415; unlike its successor it had colourful floor mosaics and external sculpture, of which fragments survive, among them a frieze of 12 lambs representing the 12 apostles.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Theodosius II</b> decreed in 435 that all pagan temples in the Eastern Roman Empire be closed, it was closed as a temple at some point in the 5th century, and Athena’s cult image was looted by one of the emperors and taken to Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/work/parthenon">Parthenon · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Marcian, a domesticus who had served the commanders Ardabur and Aspar for fifteen years, was elected and inaugurated emperor of the East after the death of <b>Theodosius II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=432" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Theodosius II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=432">What was here in 432 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
